> I wonder if Lavaan handles multilevel models (mixed-effects, hierarchical, random effect models)? It is implied here <http://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/10996/r-package-for-multilevel-structural-equation-modeling>,

No, not in the general sense.  The post refers to partitioning the covariance matrix into a Level-1 and a Level-2 covariance matrices, then fitting them both using a "multiple-group" model (where each "group" is a hierarchical level).  Any SEM software can do this, but you need to figure out how to correctly calculate the covariance matrices at each level first.  The other arbitrary MLM that can be run in any SEM software is a latent growth curve, which under certain constraints is equivalent to a MLM fit to longitudinal data (repeated observations nested within individuals).

> If not, do you have a plan to introduce this anytime soon?

>From what I understand, Yves intends to eventually implement Rabe-Hasketh & Skrondal's GLLAMM in lavaan, but it will be a loooong time before he is able to finish programming it.

http://lavaan.ugent.be/development.html

